1
00:00:01,610 --> 00:00:05,315
Bluebikes is Greater Boston’s bike-share system.

2
00:00:05,365 --> 00:00:10,780
Rebalancing moves bikes from surplus stations to stations running short.

3
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:19,000
We plan monthly full-time and seasonal crews for 2026, including hiring dates and contract
starts.

4
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:21,805
A crew means a driver and van.

5
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:26,515
Baseline means modeled staffing, not actual deployment.

6
00:00:26,675 --> 00:00:33,575
Python uses 2024–2025 trips to estimate workload and plan crews.

7
00:00:33,625 --> 00:00:39,240
January–September 2026 trips are held out for backtesting.

8
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:43,200
API calls obtain OpenAI responses.

9
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:47,560
Prompt engineering defines which event facts to extract.

10
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:54,125
Structured output gives validated fields for human review; unknowns should stay unknown.

11
00:00:54,175 --> 00:00:59,370
People supply demand assumptions; Python calculates the plan.

12
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:04,845
This demo uses a saved response from a real OpenAI API call.

13
00:01:04,895 --> 00:01:07,240
There is no new call now.

14
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:11,790
Here is the original Marathon excerpt beside its extraction.

15
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,300
April twentieth is stated.

16
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:18,775
Boston is the route endpoint, not the whole event area.

17
00:01:18,825 --> 00:01:21,940
Thirty thousand refers to athletes.

18
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:24,720
Spectators remain unknown.

19
00:01:24,770 --> 00:01:28,080
Evidence and human annotations stay visible.

20
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:34,840
I confirm the reviewed facts, then explicitly apply 3,000 added handling actions.

21
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:42,480
This is an uncalibrated retrospective scenario, not OpenAI’s demand prediction or a measured
event effect.

22
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:47,250
Boston’s April full-time crews change from 12 to 13.

23
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:54,120
Monthly crew cash cost rises from $72,000 to $78,000.

24
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:56,920
The hiring decision is in March.

25
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:00,905
This is April’s cost, not an annual total.

26
00:02:00,955 --> 00:02:08,775
Our base conversion assumes one handling action per course bike; 120 bikes per shift stays
fixed.

27
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:12,095
Both geographic panels show April.

28
00:02:12,145 --> 00:02:18,595
Dots mark stations; bubbles compare modeled city crews, not bases or routes.

29
00:02:18,645 --> 00:02:22,915
Nine other cities share one pool, shown separately.

30
00:02:23,075 --> 00:02:28,280
Review found unsupported dates and participants mistaken for spectators.

31
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:33,745
The three supplemental cases are evaluation only and cannot enter plans.

32
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:40,135
Our strongest feature preserves failures while connecting text to staffing and hiring
decisions.

33
00:02:40,185 --> 00:02:46,185
Satoshi reviewed seven cases and conditionally accepted eight disclosed assumptions.

34
00:02:46,235 --> 00:02:49,840
Vinay reviewed documentation and usability.

35
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:53,815
Uncovered proxy work is not measured lost trips.
